\section{Discussion and limits}
\label{sec:discussion}

\paragraph{What to do with a budget of $k$.}
For an evaluator with MTEB-like coverage, choosing the first two or three benchmarks by the factor model's design criterion is clearly better than choosing them at random (about 1.9 points of RMSE). From about eight on, random selection is practically as good, and what is left is the floor: about 8 benchmarks bring a new model within 2 points of what all of its other scores predict, 32 to 50 within 1 point, and no number we tried resolves neighbouring models. These numbers are for held-out organisations on this matrix; on the Open LLM Leaderboard design is practically better for the first three to five of 39 subtasks, and the numbers in points do not carry over. To size $k$ for a new matrix, the rule of Section~\ref{sec:method} estimates it from the historical models alone; it held within one grid step for held-out groups on both matrices and did not under a shift in strength or time.

\paragraph{Limits.}
A model's observed cells are the tasks its authors chose to run, which may differ from the tasks a new model's evaluator can run. The temporal and strength splits rest on 13 and 22 models, so what they say about extrapolation is indicative. The cause of the factor $2.5$ between the measured and predicted floor is not identified, and we do not claim that a better inference rule would lower the error. The greedy rule does not find the best run set of its own criterion at $k\le3$ (Appendix~\ref{app:further}), and our one adaptive baseline (S7) is a neighbour-tree rule that loses to the batch design on synthetic data, so we do not show that adaptive designs cannot do better. The second matrix holds small open models evaluated in one harness, its families are a heuristic that can leak across fine-tunes, it has no temporal split, and its largest $k$ is a third of $N$; what it shows for language models is limited to that leaderboard. The risk of shorter run sets is that an inferred score is read as a measurement, which a floor of about five points does not support.

\paragraph{Code and data.}
\label{sec:release}
The derived matrix, the code that produces every number, table and figure, the result files and notes for reproducing them are released with the paper. The raw scores are the public MTEB results repository (CC0-1.0) at commit \texttt{6dabbcc68c28ea5f180ce7df79347b3ca9cc6383}; the second matrix is built from the per-subtask results of the Open LLM Leaderboard supplied with the task.
